 Congress in place slaps some  232 tariffs on petroleum imports  Congress a Democratic Congress in both  the House and the Senate said no and  pass legislation to undo that action  President Carter vetoed that action both  houses of Congress then overrode that  veto that's the kind of thinking we need  to be involved in that's exactly what we  need to do bob corker gave a speech  about a month ago I believe that was on  the question of tariffs and he put it in  the context of we don't pass anything  here we don't pass amendments we don't  do any what the hell are we doing if we  actually voted on this it would pass if  we had an actual sense of the Senate  more than a non-binding resolution it  would probably pass and yet it doesn't  get offered what is going on with that  it's a problem this is supposed to be  the world's greatest deliberative  legislative body I'm not even sure what  that means anymore but what it's  supposed to mean is that it's a place  with theoretically unlimited debate it's  a place where anyone can offer up an  amendment anyone can offer up  legislation and have it voted on and not  just gimmicky legislation not just  something that expresses the sense of  the Senate in some abstract way but  actual legislation that's what we need  to do and we especially need to do that  here in the area of trade  it just feels like Congress has been in  a long pretty steady descent in terms of  abdicating its responsibility on any  number of things just pass a budget okay  we can we pass a normal budget with  appropriation bills and things like that  you came into the Senate as a reformer  as a young voice you heard tea party guy  back when there was a tea party and you  know you you toppled the establishment  bringing some energy and some kick into  the place partly from a sense of we need  to have more rich oversight or the  executive branch what's going on why is  there is it just our the incentive  is so strong to not do anything and just  have a single up-and-down vote on a on a  you know a cromnibus bill at the end of  the year what explains the way it  doesn't work it's the drive for  perpetual reelection that's at the  center these six years realize yes yes  exactly but the more members of Congress  can avoid making difficult decisions the  easier it is for members of Congress  whether an they're in the house or the  Senate Republican or Democrat the easier  it is for them to achieve perpetual  re-election and so that's why you've  seen this odd confluence of events  you've seen the centralization of power  power being taken from the people move  to Washington but then within Washington  there's a second step where the power is  transferred from the people selected  lawmakers in the House and Senate or to  unelected unaccountable bureaucrats in  the executive branch who are not  accountable to anyone they're not  accountable to the voters through  election they're not really even most of  the time accountable to anyone who is in  turn accountable to the people through  election this is bad for everyone else  but it's good for us small handful of  politicians who find that this process  makes it easier for them to get  reelected and it's bad for the American  people it is by the way very good for a  small handful of experts in Washington  DC will become wealthy off of the  complexity that surrounds Washington DC  you raised a related point by the way  and referring to the way we spend money  by the way we pass appropriations bills  more often than not we don't have a  regular process in which individual  members are allowed to craft legislation  or change the way that our spending  bills are put together we're instead  given a pile of papers sometimes a  couple thousand pages long a few hours  before the expiration of a spending  deadline that's wrong when there's no  opportunity for debate for discussion  for amendment for individual members to  improve it you've effectively  disenfranchised almost 300 million  Americans unless you're one of those  lucky four members named perhaps Ryan  Pelosi McConnell or Schumer then you're  being shut out of the process that's not  right so it's malpractice it's it's  stunning that it happens even though we  can sort of understand the  you are trying to do something with  criminal justice reform which is an  issue that the administration has  expressed perhaps surprising to some  interest at least in some of the prison  reform aspects to that you seem to be  optimistic about some work that you're  doing to match up with a House bill can  you talk us through what's what's in  front of you sure in the last Congress  we proposed something called the  sentencing reform and Corrections Act  passed through the Judiciary Committee  with a bipartisan supermajority vote of  15 to 5 oh this year's version of it  passed out of this Congress's Senate  Judiciary Committee with a vote of 16 to  5 it contains two basic provisions two  basic titles one title dealing with  sentencing reform making some necessary  adjustments to minimum mandatory  sentence laws that sometimes result in  excessive prison sentences for low-level  nonviolent drug offenders in particular  the other title deals with prison reform  preparing people to be released into the  general population helping to make sure  that they're ready to go back out that  they've got the skills the coping  mechanisms and some of the basic  educational inputs necessary to make  sure that they stay away from crime the  House of Representatives has passed the  prison reform portion of that I believe  that can pass the Senate but it can pass  the Senate most likely only if we add a  few sentencing reform provisions to it I  think if we do that we can get most or  all the Republicans and most of the  Democrats to join with us we could get  this passed this year and what's your  sense of the house accepting some of the  sentencing reform guideline I think it's  very high because these are common-sense  revisions that we're making this is not  going to result in opening the  floodgates and letting out a whole host  of dangerous felons onto the streets  these are relatively modest adjustments  so that we can get away from this  Byzantine labyrinth of laws that  sometimes results in some people going  to prison for 55 years for selling three  dime bags of marijuana  over a 72-hour period just because they  had a gun on their person at the time  the President or the administration  people around him have indicated at  least less when I looked at it which is  a longer girl  when you did obviously they're  interested in the prison reform the  sentence reform not so much Jeff  Sessions your former colleague I don't  think it's a big fan of that and a lot  of other criminal justice reforms that I  might like to see what is your sense of  being able to talk the administration  into agreeing to take on some of that  sentencing reform that you would also  like to say first of all I think it  would be a mistake to suggest that  everyone close to the president is  opposed to any idea related to  sentencing reform secondly I think  there's an understanding that prison  reform is necessary and if the price of  doing that involves some modest  sentencing reform so be it let's get it  done that's why I expect victory  sometime between now and a few months  from now that's that's very positive  news and indeed you think the president  is enjoying the applause that he  receives when he uses as a commutation  and and pardoning power there absolutely  i I think he enjoys doing that and I  think he's used that power pretty well  from what I can discern I I think he's  identified some instances in which  people have been mistreated by a large  overpowering federal government one that  has sometimes overextended itself  particularly in the area of criminal law  enforcement last two subject before I  let you go you're interested in passing  a kind of thing having to do the  antiquities act not allowing a president  to willy-nilly use the antiquities act  to you to federalize more national park  eyes more land instead of me describing  it why don't you and tell us what you're  up with of that sure the federal  government owns close to 30 percent of  all the land mass in the United States  it's not evenly distributed at all most  of its in the western United States in  every state east of Colorado the federal  government owns less than 15% of the  land  you've been many if not most States it's  a percentage that can be reckoned in the  single digits in every state west of  Colorado the federal government owns a  lot more than 15% of the land in the  case of my state Utah the federal  government owns two-thirds of the land  that limits our ability to control our  own access to that land what we can use  it for federal land itself can be  accessed with some local in  put as long as it's within some  categories but under the antiquities act  passed some 110 hundred and twelve years  ago the president has the unilateral  power to designate an area as a National  Monument once it's made a monument that  federal land is put into a higher level  of restriction it's much more difficult  to access much more difficult for local  populations to determine the rules  surrounding access to that land Utah has  fallen victim to this and in fact we've  been the victim of millions of acres of  monument designations by presidents who  have done so made these designations  contrary to the will of local  populations and so it's one of the  reasons why I'm proposing that Utah be  given a special protection a protection  analogous to what Wyoming already has an  Alaska already has and Utah's case my  bill would say that if a president wants  to designate any more of our land as a  National Monument it can't take effect  until you first have approval from  Congress and you also have approval from  our state legislature I don't think  that's too much to ask  especially when America's poor and  middle class particularly poor middle  class people in Utah are most deeply  affected by this land we shouldn't be  subjected to any more of this without  our consent the counter-argument to that  you'll hear from environmentalists is  that you are preventing the president  from protecting some of the most  beautiful land in the country which Utah  it undoubtedly is and therefore are you  just going to strip-mine it for whatever  nefarious reasons that you're doing  what's your response I could not  disagree with that statement more  strongly look no one wants to protect  that land more than those who live  closest to it suggesting that we want to  do this so that we can drill under  delicate arch or double arch or some  other national landmark some other  beautiful place is absurd and it's  contrary to the facts on the ground the  fact is that no one cares more about  that land and the people living closest  to it but they understandably  justifiably want to have some input into  how they access that land rather than  having those decisions made thousands of  miles away  by a government bureaucrat accountable  to no one very good well thank you very  much Senator Mike Lee for future Supreme  Court justice Mike Lee for talking to us  for reason I am that well  [Music]
 

